: \"12 data rows, 12 distinct ordered (k1,k2) pairs, maximum rows per pair = 1\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,shared,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,shared,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,shared,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,shared,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,shared,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,shared,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,shared,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,shared,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,shared,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,shared,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,shared,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,shared,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The workflow feeds one value per row into each sample of a row-independence-assuming procedure (ttest_ind), so the number of entries per sample equals the number of distinct source items (12 each); no unit is entered repeatedly.\", \"observed_value\": \"left = 12 values, right = 12 values, one per distinct (k1,k2) source item\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 7, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nresult = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The reported degrees of freedom equal 22 = 12 + 12 - 2, corroborating that each sample consists of exactly 12 observations, matching the 12 distinct source items rather than an inflated repeated-unit count.\", \"observed_value\": \"df = 22.0\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"df=np.float64(22.0)\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"The `tag` column holds the identical literal value \\\"shared\\\" on every row, which superficially suggests a common grouping/unit across all rows; however, the task designates the ordered (k1,k2) pair — not `tag` — as the source-item identifier, and `tag` is never read by the workflow.\", \"observed_value\": \"tag == \\\"shared\\\" for all 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 3, \"quoted_text\": \"k1,k2,tag,a,b\\nu01,v01,shared,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,shared,2.0,4.0\"}, {\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item\"}]}, {\"description\": \"Columns a and b are drawn from the same physical row (hence the same source item) yet are compared with an independent-samples test, so the two samples are not mutually independent. This is a paired-vs-independent design mismatch, distinct from the in-scope class of one unit contributing multiple rows to a single sample.\", \"observed_value\": \"ttest_ind applied to within-row paired columns a and b\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 7, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nresult = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"To falsify a \\\"no issue\\\" verdict I searched for any repetition of the independent unit: I enumerated all 12 ordered (k1,k2) pairs and found no duplicate, so no unit enters either sample more than once, and df=22 confirms sample sizes of 12 = number of units. I then tried the strongest alternative reading that would make the issue present — treating the uniform `tag` value \\\"shared\\\" as the true unit label, which would make all 12 rows one repeated unit — but the task explicitly and exclusively defines the source item as the ordered (k1,k2) pair, and `tag` is never referenced in the workflow, so that reading contradicts the governing task rather than supporting it. The remaining defect I can substantiate (independent-samples test on within-row paired columns) is a different failure mode and outside the named issue class, so I could not demonstrate the in-scope issue.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}, {\"case_id\": \"case:ede79e7dd2b73963ae43\", \"verdict\": \"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope\", \"bounded_statement\": null, \"root_cause\": null, \"issue_class\": null, \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"All 12 staged rows carry pairwise-distinct ordered (k1,k2) pairs, so under the task's unit definition each source item contributes exactly one row and no unit is entered repeatedly into the procedure.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 data rows, 12 distinct ordered (k1,k2) pairs, maximum rows per pair = 1\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The auxiliary `tag` column is also unique per row (t01..t12), so no secondary column in the staged data groups multiple rows under a common label either.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 distinct tag values for 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The workflow contributes exactly one observation per row to each sample of ttest_ind, and the reported df=22=12+12-2 confirms each sample has 12 entries, equal to the number of distinct source items.\", \"observed_value\": \"df = 22.0 with 12 units per sample\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 7, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nresult = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}, {\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"df=np.float64(22.0)\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"The workflow applies no grouping, aggregation, or unit-level collapsing whatsoever — it passes raw rows straight into the test. Such a workflow would silently commit the in-scope error if the staged data ever contained duplicated ordered pairs; the verdict rests entirely on this particular staged file containing none.\", \"observed_value\": \"staged = rows (no deduplication or per-unit aggregation)\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 5, \"end_line\": 6, \"quoted_text\": \"rows = list(csv.DictReader(Path(\\\"inputs/data.csv\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").splitlines()))\\nstaged = rows\"}]}, {\"description\": \"Values a and b come from the same row and therefore the same source item, yet are compared as two independent samples; the two samples are not independent of each other. This is a paired-design mismatch rather than repeated entry of a unit into one sample.\", \"observed_value\": \"ttest_ind applied to within-row paired columns a and b\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 7, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nresult = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"To falsify a \\\"no issue\\\" verdict I enumerated every ordered (k1,k2) pair looking for a repeat that would inflate the effective sample size; all 12 are distinct, and the secondary `tag` column is likewise unique per row, so no visible column groups rows into a shared unit. I cross-checked the arithmetic: df=22 implies 12 observations per sample, exactly the number of distinct units, leaving no room for a hidden duplicate. I also considered whether the workflow's complete absence of grouping logic constitutes the issue on its own, but the named class requires repeated authorized entry of a unit to be present in the produced result, and with no duplicate rows staged, no such repetition occurred. The one defect I can substantiate — an independent-samples test on within-row paired columns — falls outside the named class.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}, {\"case_id\": \"case:f170479fec4de0e31a85\", \"verdict\": \"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope\", \"bounded_statement\": null, \"root_cause\": null, \"issue_class\": null, \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"Under the task's definition of a source item as the distinct ordered (k1,k2) pair, all 12 staged rows are distinct units (u01,v01 through u12,v12); no ordered pair recurs, so no unit contributes multiple rows.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 data rows, 12 distinct ordered (k1,k2) pairs, maximum rows per pair = 1\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The workflow builds each sample with exactly one value per row, so each sample passed to mannwhitneyu (a row-independence-assuming procedure) contains 12 entries corresponding one-to-one with the 12 distinct source items.\", \"observed_value\": \"left = 12 values, right = 12 values, one per distinct source item\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 6, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in rows]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in rows]\\nresult = mannwhitneyu(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The reported U statistic of 60.5 is consistent with sample sizes n1=n2=12 (maximum possible U = 144, midpoint 72), corroborating that neither sample was inflated by duplicated units.\", \"observed_value\": \"statistic = 60.5, compatible with n1 = n2 = 12\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"MannwhitneyuResult(statistic=np.float64(60.5), pvalue=np.float64(0.5243792697676437))\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"The workflow reads rows and feeds them directly to the test with no per-unit aggregation or duplicate check, so it provides no structural safeguard against the in-scope error; the absence of the issue depends solely on the staged file containing no repeated ordered pair.\", \"observed_value\": \"no grouping or deduplication between CSV read and mannwhitneyu call\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 5, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"rows = list(csv.DictReader(Path(\\\"inputs/data.csv\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").splitlines()))\\nleft = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in rows]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in rows]\\nresult = mannwhitneyu(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The a and b samples are measured on the same rows and therefore the same source items, but mannwhitneyu is used in its independent-samples form rather than a paired rank test; this is a paired-vs-independent mismatch, not repeated entry of a unit into a single sample.\", \"observed_value\": \"mannwhitneyu (independent) applied to within-row paired columns\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 3, \"end_line\": 3, \"quoted_text\": \"from scipy.stats import mannwhitneyu\"}, {\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 8, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"result = mannwhitneyu(left, right)\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"To falsify a \\\"no issue\\\" verdict I looked for any ordered (k1,k2) pair appearing on more than one row, which would mean an authorized unit was entered repeatedly into a row-independence-assuming procedure; all 12 pairs are distinct and the `tag` values are unique as well, so no visible column clusters rows under a shared unit. I also checked the reported statistic for evidence of an inflated n: U=60.5 sits within the range for n1=n2=12, consistent with 12 units per sample rather than any duplicated entries. Finally I considered whether the workflow's lack of any deduplication step could itself demonstrate the class, but the class requires the repeated entry to have actually occurred in the produced result, and with no duplicate units staged it did not. The substantiable defect here (independent-samples rank test on paired within-row columns) is outside the named class.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}]}","ttft_ms":17316,"ttft_stream_ms":2146,"time_to_request_ms":325,"type":"result","duration_ms":74373,"uuid":"66a4b15f-b5ca-4ccc-adf0-5e6f98d96758"}
